\details{
The signal, such as a line intensity from \code{\link[=line_intensities]{line_intensities()}} or a line
ratio, is modeled as a straight line or a second-degree polynomial of the
concentration, fitted by (weighted) least squares. Weights \code{"1/x"} or
\code{"1/x2"} suit signals whose variance grows with concentration, which is
common in LIBS.

\strong{Figures of merit.} The sensitivity is the slope of the curve at zero
concentration. The limits of detection and quantification are
\eqn{LOD = 3.3\,\sigma / S} and \eqn{LOQ = 10\,\sigma / S} (ICH, 2005),
where \eqn{S} is the sensitivity and \eqn{\sigma} the standard deviation
of the signal of a blank, estimated from
\itemize{
\item \code{"residual"}: the residual standard deviation of the fit (default
without \code{blank});
\item \code{"intercept"}: the standard error of the intercept;
\item \code{"blank"}: the standard deviation of replicate \code{blank} signals
(default when given).
